# Orb architecture

The Limbo orb is one WebGL point cloud in `LimboScene.tsx` (a `Points` object, about 5,600 particles). Each particle has a `home` on a procedural sphere. They are one group, so moving the group moves every particle. This is the same idea as the Dala brain (particle cloud whose homes came from a shape), not an SVG and not a solid mesh.

Current behavior — pointer follow, singularity on a world button, heartbeat, shed, stray pull — is specified in `site-architecture.mdc`. Keep it. The loop that implements it runs on the CPU inside `useFrame`. `SHELL` and `LOST` are per-particle state: a stray stores a velocity and an escape timer, and landing rewrites `home` to the arrival point.

## Do this next

Scroll, a turn, and a flight move the **group**, not the particles one by one. Write the target into a ref and apply it in the existing `useFrame`, the way the pointer already moves the group. Driving `position` or `rotation` from React state on scroll re-renders the scene every frame and will stutter.

An explosion or a sound wave is an offset away from `home`, then the spring back. The shed stray is the small version of this. Add that offset in the current loop.

A morph (sphere to speaker, sphere to energy cloud, brain to bulb to planet) is a second home list of the **same count**. Ease each `home` toward the new list. The shell already chases `home`, so the shape change is smooth. Position, shape, and sprite ease over the same stretch of time. No hard cut.

## World entries

The music and software buttons do not open `/music` or `/software`. Those pages stay in the repo.

Enter sound plays on the Limbo page, from the button or from a horizontal scroll toward the left. Scrolling back toward the right reverses that flight: the chrome returns and the speaker eases into the orb again. That horizontal wheel is cancelled so the browser does not treat it as back/forward and open `/music`. Vertical scrolling stays on the studio zoom in `ArrivalPage` and does not open a world. The limbo chrome slides off to the right. The background stays. The singularity leaves the cursor and settles at a fixed point on the left, and it does not follow the pointer after that. On the way, homes blend from the sphere into a speaker whose cone faces right. Once it has landed, each beat pushes a wave of particles out of the cone toward the right side of the screen, where the music UI will go. A horizontal scroll toward the right is the software direction. Enter code does not navigate and has no flight yet.

## What not to refactor

A `.gltf` mesh only supplies a new list of homes. It does not explode, scroll, or morph. Keep the procedural sphere until a shape actually needs authored points.

A vertex shader is a later upgrade for a denser look (instanced triangles, cursor glow, curl noise), and only after a measured frame drop. It is not required for scroll or morph. Stateless effects — swell, cursor push, explosion offset — are formulas of home and time and can move to the GPU. The stray lifecycle cannot: it remembers velocity, a timer, and a rewritten home. That needs a simulation buffer, not a one-pass shader. Leave it in JavaScript.

Reduced motion freezes the spin, the follow, the shed, the heartbeat, the collapse, and any flight or morph.
